and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L31: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL32: \nL33: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. L34: \nL35: cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L36: \nL37: Research\nL38: ## Explore our many areas of focus\nL39: \nL40: cite1†Explore all research areas L41: \nL42: Applied AI & sciences\nL43: \nL44: cite2†Earth AI cite3†Health AI cite4†Science AI cite5†Sustainability & crisis resilience L45: \nL46: Foundational ML & algorithms\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Algorithms & theory cite7†Information retrieval cite8†Machine intelligence cite9†Machine perception cite10†Natural language processing L49: \nL50: People, systems & quantum AI\nL51: cite11†Human-computer interaction and visualization cite12†Networking cite13†Quantum AI cite14†Responsible AI cite15†Anti abuse cite16†Software engineering cite17†Software systems L52: \nL53: Learn More\nL54: \nL55: cite18†Publications cite19†Projects L56: \nL57: Resources\nL58: ## Building a collaborative ecosystem\nL59: \nL60: cite20†Datasets Access high-quality datasets to accelerate your research. cite21†Tools & services Explore our latest AI models and products. cite22†Open source Discover open-source code and collaborate with the community. L61: \nL62: cite30†Conferences & events L63: \nL64: Careers\nL65: ## Shaping the future together\nL66: \nL67: cite23†See all programs L68: \nL69: cite24†Faculty programs Participating in the academic research community through meaningful engagement with university faculty. cite25†Student programs Supporting the next generation of researchers through a wide range of programming. cite26†R&D programs Collaborating with industry and academic experts to support foundational science and innovation. cite27†Locations Find your place in our global offices and research labs. L70: \nL71: cite31†Blog L72: \nL73: About\nL74: ## Translating discovery into real-world impact\nL75: \nL76: cite28†People Our researchers drive advancements in computer science through both fundamental and applied research. cite29†Teams Collaborative groups tackling the world's most challenging AI problems. L77: \nL78: cite32†Google Research L79: \nL80: cite33†Google AI Learn about all our AI†ai.google cite34†Google DeepMind Explore the frontier of AI†deepmind.google cite35†Google Labs Try our AI experiments†labs.google L81: \nL82: [Button: Research ]\nL83: \nL84: [Button: Resources ]\nL85: \nL86: cite30†Conferences & events L87: [Button: Careers ]\nL88: \nL89: cite31†Blog L90: \nL91: [Button: About ]\nL92: \nL93: [Input: Search]\nL94: \nL95: Search\nL96: \nL97: cite36†Image: Deep researcher with test-time diffusion†storage.googleapis.com L98: # Deep researcher with test-time diffusion\nL99: \nL100: September 19, 2025\nL101: \nL102: Rujun Han and Chen-Yu Lee, Research Scientists, Google Cloud\nL103: \nL104: We introduce Test-Time Diffusion Deep Researcher (TTD-DR), a framework that uses a Deep Research agent to draft and revise its own drafts using high-quality retrieved information. This approach achieves new state-of-the-art results in writing long-form research reports and completing complex reasoning tasks.\nL105: ## Quick links\nL106: \nL107:   * cite37†Paper†arxiv.org L108: \nL109: The recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have fueled the emergence of cite38†deep research†openai.com (DR) agents. These agents demonstrate remarkable capabilities, including the generation of cite39†novel ideas†arxiv.org , efficient cite40†information retrieval†arxiv.org , experimental execution, and the subsequent drafting of comprehensive cite41†reports†arxiv.org and cite42†academic papers†arxiv.org .\nL110: Currently, most cite43†public DR agents†github.com use a variety of clever techniques to improve their results, like cite44†performing reasoning via chain-of-thought or cite45†generating multiple answers†openreview.net and selecting the best one. While they've made impressive progress, they often bolt different tools together without considering the iterative nature of human research.\nL111: They're missing the key process (i.e., planning, drafting, researching, and iterating based on feedback) on which people rely when writing a paper about a complex topic. A key part of that revision process is to do more research to cite46†find missing information or strengthen your arguments†www.emerald.com .\nL112: This human pattern is surprisingly similar to the mechanism of cite47†retrieval†proceedings.mlr.press -augmented diffusion models that start with a “noisy” or messy output and gradually refine it into a high-quality result. What if an AI agent's rough draft is the noisy version, and a search tool acts as the denoising step that cleans it up with new facts?\nL113: Today we introduce cite37†Test-Time Diffusion Deep Researcher†arxiv.org (TTD-DR), a DR agent that imitates the way humans do research. To our knowledge, TTD-DR is the first research agent that models research report writing as a diffusion process, where a messy first draft is gradually polished into a high-quality final version. We introduce two new algorithms that work together to enable TTD-DR.\nL114: First, component-wise optimization via cite48†self-evolution†arxiv.org enhances the quality of each step in the research workflow. Then, report-level refinement via cite49†denoising with retrieval†arxiv.org applies newly retrieved information to revise and improve the report draft. We demonstrate that TTD-DR achieves state-of-the-art results on long-form report writing and multi-hop reasoning tasks.\nL115: ## Test-Time Diffusion Deep Researcher\nL116: TTD-DR is designed to take a user query as input and then create a preliminary draft that serves as an evolving foundation to guide the research plan. This evolving draft is iteratively refined using a denoising with retrieval process (report-level refinement) that takes the information it finds and uses it to improve the draft at each step. This happens in a continuous loop that improves the report with each cycle.\n"
